// @error list element expects a value, got void
// CONTRACT (must-error): a list literal's element slot demands a value, and a void call has
// none. The ANNOTATED spelling (`const xs: i32[] = [pv()]`) is already refused, by the
// binding's own assignability message — `cannot assign void[] to 'xs' of type i32[]` — so
// this is the UN-annotated twin, where the element type comes from the element itself and
// nothing compares it to anything. The element is poisoned to the error type once reported,
// so the annotated spelling keeps its single message rather than gaining a second one.
//
// A VERDICT pin: the defect is a program that does not build (the emitted module failed to
// validate — there was no value to store in the element), and the fix makes it fail at the
// check tier instead. There is no correct run for a `@log` to compare against.
function pv() { print(1) }
const xs = [pv()]
print(xs.length)
